%T A400631 8868,10698,12850,15242,18018,21076,24584,28420,32778,37512,42846,
%U A400631 48608,55052,61980,69678,77918,87022,96730,107400,118740,131146,144290,158610,173740,190160,207466,226182,245862,267078,289340
%N A400631 Number of semi-meanders with n crossings and winding number n-6, for n >= 6.
%C A400631 A semi-meander is a semi-infinite, self-avoiding, directed curve that crosses a line one or more times.
%C A400631 The winding number of a semi-meander is the hypothetical minimum number of self-crossings that would be created if a ray were to start at the source of the semi-infinite directed curve and extend to infinity without crossing the line.
%C A400631 The maximum winding number of a semi-meander with n crossings is n-2. Thus a(n) counts semi-meanders whose winding number is 4 less than the maximum.
%C A400631 Equivalent to a closed curve (a loop) transversely crossing a ray, considered up to continuous deformation fixing the ray, with a winding number equal to the number of crossings that would be created if the fixed end of the ray were extended to form a line.
%C A400631 Analogized to a river (a semi-meander, meander, or loop) crossing a road (a ray, line, or line segment).
%C A400631 The third diagonal of A400429, reading diagonals from the maximal-winding edge.
%H A400631 Hunter Hogan, <a href="/A400631/b400631.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 6..10005</a>
%H A400631 Philippe Di Francesco, Olivier Golinelli and Emmanuel Guitter, <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/hep-th/9607039">Meanders: A Direct Enumeration Approach</a>, arXiv:hep-th/9607039, 1996 (preprint).
%H A400631 Philippe Di Francesco, Olivier Golinelli and Emmanuel Guitter, <a href="https://doi.org/10.1016/S0550-3213(96)00505-6">Meanders: A Direct Enumeration Approach</a>, Nuclear Physics B 482 (1996), no. 3, 497-535.
%H A400631 Hunter Hogan, <a href="https://github.com/hunterhogan/mapFolding">mapFolding: Create, alter, and optimize algorithms to count meanders, stamp foldings, and map foldings</a>, GitHub repository, 2026.
%H A400631 Joe Sawada and Roy Li, <a href="https://doi.org/10.37236/2404">Stamp Foldings, Semi-Meanders, and Open Meanders: Fast Generation Algorithms</a>, The Electronic Journal of Combinatorics, Volume 19 No. 2 (2012), #P43 (16 pages).
%H A400631 Wenlin Zhang and Haobo Ma, <a href="/A400429/a400429.pdf">Semi-meanders with a fixed winding deficit: the third and fourth diagonals of A400429</a>, 2026.
%F A400631 a(n) = T(n,floor(n/2)-2), where T is the triangle A400429.
%F A400631 More generally, if D_j is the j-th diagonal of A400429, then 2*j is its least index.
%F A400631 For this sequence, j = 3 and 2*j = 6.
%F A400631 D_j(2*j)   = a(6) = A005315(j)   = A005316(2*j-1).
%F A400631 D_j(2*j+1) = a(7) = A005315(j+1) = A005316(2*j+1) = D_{j+1}(2*j+2) = A400632(8).
%F A400631 a(n) = P(n) + (-1)^n*E(n) + C_3(n).
%F A400631 P(n) = (78*n^4 + 144*n^3 - 1650*n^2 - 27576*n + 107959) / 1728.
%F A400631 E(n) = (-18*n^2 + 72*n - 189) / 576.
%F A400631 m_3(t) = 2*cos(2*Pi*t/3).
%F A400631 C_3(n) = -2*m_3(n)/27.
%e A400631 Row n=10 of A400429 is 262,752,340,50,2, corresponding to winding numbers 0,2,4,6,8. Since n-6=4, a(10)=340, which is the third-to-last value.
%Y A400631 Third diagonal of A400429. Cf. A005315, A005316, A400630, A400632, A400633, A400634, A400635, A400636, A400637, A400638.
